          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does \"with\" require the two terms to sit next to each other in the documentation, or is it an index convention?",
          "answer": "The sources I searched say the \"with\" rule is an index convention, not a matter of wording or placement. They do not say whether the two terms must sit next to each other in the record, so I can't answer that part.\n\n**What the sources say:** In the US clinical modification of ICD-10 (ICD-10-CM), when the Alphabetic Index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification reads this as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes the relationship. The record does not have to state it. The presumption applies only to pairings the index actually lists, and it is overridden if the documentation says the two conditions are unrelated. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*; also *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*, which gives type 2 diabetes with chronic kidney disease as an example.)\n\n**What the sources do not say:** Nothing I retrieved addresses adjacency, sentence placement, or wording. The passages describe the rule as tied to the index entry, which suggests the link comes from the index and not from how the note is phrased. That is an inference, not a stated rule. For the exact coding instructions, check the current ICD-10-CM Alphabetic Index and the Tabular List notes directly, or ask a trained medical coder.\n\nThe sources also describe the US version only. The WHO's international ICD-10 keeps the same chapter structure but differs in detail, so I can't confirm the WHO version applies this rule the same way.\n\nThis is general educational information, not advice on how any specific record should be coded.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
